import json

REWRITES = {

"01-08": """Jesus didn't sympathize with your wounds from a distance—He absorbed them. Every sin, every shame, every broken thing in your story was transferred onto His body on the cross. That's not poetry. That's your present reality.

This is who God is toward you: He heals by entering the disease Himself. He didn't send instructions from heaven—He came as the solution. He walked into the darkest corner of your suffering and stayed until your freedom was complete.

On the morning when your past feels permanent, when shame insists there's no way forward—this truth cuts through it all. You are not still paying for your mistakes. Jesus paid them. His suffering was surgical, specific, and completely sufficient.

You don't have to earn this healing. You don't have to reach a certain level of goodness before it takes effect. "By his wounds you have been healed" is past tense—finished work, fully done. You simply receive what He's already given.

You can trust this because He bore it in His actual body. On a real cross, with real pain, Jesus carried everything you deserved. The cost was too high to be anything but absolute truth. His wounds are proof that your debt is gone.

You are not damaged goods. You are not beyond repair. You are the person for whom the Son of God took every wound—and those wounds are the permanent proof that your wholeness is already secured.

Jesus, thank You for bearing what I deserved so I could receive what You earned. By Your wounds I am healed—not someday, but now. I receive that healing into every broken place in my heart, my mind, and my body. You bore it all so I never have to. Amen.""",

"02-08": """"Take heart, my son; your sins are forgiven." Jesus said this before healing the man's legs. That's not an accident—it's a revelation of what God considers your deepest need.

He saw a paralyzed man and immediately looked past his body to his soul. Before addressing the visible problem, He healed the invisible one. This is who God is toward you: not a doctor treating symptoms, but a Father restoring relationship.

The man never asked for forgiveness. He came hoping for physical healing. But Jesus, with one look, knew what this man needed most—not just new legs, but a clean slate. That's how completely He knows you too.

His love works the same way in your life. He sees past your presenting problem to your deepest need. His first move is always toward your heart—not your performance, not your record, but you. Forgiven, free, and called His own.

You can trust Him because He didn't wait for this man to ask. He moved first. He always moves first. Before you can clean yourself up, before you can explain yourself, before you can prove you deserve it—He's already extended grace.

"Take heart." These words cut through every shame, every failure, every morning when guilt makes it hard to breathe. Jesus lifts your head. The verdict is already in: you are forgiven, and that changes everything.

Father, thank You for seeing past my visible struggles to my deepest need. You forgive me before I can form the words to ask. My heart is clean, my guilt is gone, and I am Yours. I take heart today, because You have made me completely new. Amen.""",

"03-08": """God names your pain before He fixes it. "Hope deferred makes the heart sick"—He doesn't tell you to stop feeling the ache of waiting. He acknowledges it, because the God who made your heart fully understands what it costs to keep hoping.

But His character doesn't just diagnose—it delivers. A longing fulfilled is a tree of life. Not a consolation prize, not an apology for the delay. A living, growing, fruit-bearing tree—which is exactly what happens when God keeps His word.

You might be in that deferred season right now. Waiting on a breakthrough, a healing, a door to open. The waiting is real. The heartache is real. But so is His promise. He has not forgotten what He's planted in your heart.

God's love doesn't abandon what it's promised. He doesn't dangle hope and then withdraw it. Every longing He placed in you was placed intentionally—and He is more committed to fulfilling it than you are to receiving it.

You can trust Him because He is a God who fulfills. Not eventually, if circumstances align. He fulfills because faithfulness is His nature. His timing is purposeful. His love doesn't plant longings it never intends to satisfy.

While you wait, let this truth hold you: you are not forgotten. You are not on the wrong path. You are in the hands of a God who is already working on what you've been praying for.

Father, my heart has grown tired from waiting, but I trust You. You see every longing. You haven't forgotten a single promise. I believe that what You've planted in me, You will bring to life—in Your perfect time. You are faithful even when I can't see it yet. Amen.""",

"04-08": """God sees opportunity where everyone else sees tragedy. When the disciples asked who sinned to cause this man's blindness, they were asking the wrong question—and Jesus came to give them a completely different way to see.

This is who God is toward you: He doesn't waste your pain. What looks like a dead end to everyone else, He sees as a doorway. What feels like punishment is often preparation—a canvas for His glory to appear in a way nobody expected.

The disciples' question was human logic at its most predictable: pain must be someone's fault. But Jesus refused that framework. He pointed to something better—not blame, but purpose. Not a reason to punish, but a reason to reveal.

You may be carrying something that you've spent years trying to explain or justify. The illness, the loss, the situation that doesn't make sense. His promise is this: where you see darkness, He sees the exact conditions His light needs to shine.

You can trust Him because He sees the whole picture. He knows where your story is going. He is not reactive to your circumstances—He is purposeful within them, working things together for a good that you can't yet see from where you're standing.

Stop asking why and start asking what. What does God want to do through this? That shift in question changes everything. It moves you from victim to vessel.

Lord, I give You my unanswered questions. You don't waste what I've been through—You redeem it. Turn my darkest seasons into the clearest testimony of Your grace. I trust that You are working in what I cannot yet understand. Amen.""",

"05-08": """Faith sees what knowledge misses. A blind man, healed by Jesus, looked the religious experts in the eye and said what they couldn't admit: "He opened my eyes." Simple. Undeniable. Unshakeable.

The Pharisees had spent their lives studying Scripture, yet they missed the miracle standing right in front of them. This man had no credentials, no religious education, no status. He just had an encounter with Jesus—and that was enough.

His simple testimony shamed their complicated theology. He didn't need to out-argue them. He just knew what had happened to him. "One thing I know: I was blind, and now I see." You can build your whole life on that kind of faith.

God's love reaches the overlooked ones first. This man was cast aside by society, considered unworthy. Yet he became the recipient of a miracle and the bearer of a testimony that outlasted the entire religious establishment of his day.

You can trust Jesus because He opens eyes—even the ones people have given up on. He doesn't require a certain pedigree or performance level before He shows up. He requires only the willingness to acknowledge what He has done.

Your testimony doesn't have to be sophisticated to be powerful. It just has to be true. The simplest faith, rooted in a real encounter with Jesus, will always outlast the cleverest arguments against Him.

Jesus, thank You that I don't need credentials to know You—I just need an encounter with You. What You've done in my life is undeniable. Open my eyes wider every day to see You more clearly. One thing I know: You changed me, and that's enough. Amen.""",

"06-08": """Beloved, when the world tries to tell you who you are, remember this moment. The religious leaders looked at this man and saw only sin and shame. They cast him out. But their rejection couldn't touch his real identity—because his identity wasn't theirs to define.

You are not who others say you are. You are not defined by your past, your mistakes, or the labels people place on you. The moment Jesus touched this man's life, his identity was sealed forever—not by performance, but by love.

The same radical love that sought out this rejected man seeks you today. Jesus doesn't love you because you have it together. He loves you because you are His. That love isn't conditional on your background or your reputation. It's rooted in His unchanging character.

When rejection comes—and it will—rest in this: their opinion cannot alter your position. You belong to the One who calls the rejected "beloved." That identity runs deeper than any wound, stronger than any accusation, more permanent than any circumstance.

You can trust Him because He always moves toward the cast-out. When everyone else walked away, Jesus went looking. That's not coincidence—that's His nature. He finds the ones others have given up on and calls them His own.

Today, let the peace of knowing who you are in Christ quiet every voice trying to convince you otherwise. You are not defined by your worst moment. You are radically, unconditionally, eternally loved.

Jesus, when the world tries to cast me out, remind me that You are always drawing me in. My identity is not up for debate—I am Yours, and that settles everything. In Your love, I find unshakeable ground. Amen.""",

"07-08": """Jesus heard that they had thrown him out—and went looking for him. He didn't wait for the man to compose himself, or find his way back, or prove he was ready. The moment this man was alone, Jesus made finding him His priority.

Your isolation doesn't intimidate Him. Your mess doesn't repel Him. When everyone else walks away, He walks toward you. He doesn't come to scold you for causing trouble. He comes to reveal Himself and offer relationship.

Notice what Jesus asks: "Do you believe in the Son of Man?" He's not asking about doctrine. He's saying, "Do you know who I am? And do you know who you are, because of who I am?" This is where peace is born—in knowing you're found.

You are not defined by who rejected you. You are defined by who came looking for you. Not marked by the doors that closed, but by the heart that opened wide to receive you. Jesus doesn't love you despite your story—He loves you through it.

You can trust Him because He is always closer than your next breath. When shame whispers you're too far gone, remember: the Son of Man knows exactly where to find you. He always has. He always will.

This is the peace that passes understanding—not the absence of rejection, but the presence of the One who claims you as His beloved no matter how far you've wandered.

Lord Jesus, thank You for seeking me out when everyone else turned away. In my loneliest moments, You draw near. In my rejection, You offer acceptance. Help me believe not just in who You are, but in who I am because You love me—radically, completely, forever. Amen.""",

"08-08": """The thief wants you to settle. Settle for anxiety instead of peace. Settle for shame instead of identity. Settle for surviving instead of thriving. But Jesus came to shatter every lie that whispers, "This is as good as it gets."

The abundant life He offers isn't about more stuff. It's about knowing, deeply, who you are and whose you are. It's waking up with unshakeable peace because you are loved by the Creator of the universe. It's walking through your day knowing your worth isn't determined by performance.

When the enemy whispers that you're too broken, too late, too far gone—remember this promise. Jesus didn't come to give you a slightly better version of your old life. He came to give you His life. His peace in the storm. His joy in the depths. His strength in your weakness.

This fullness isn't something you work toward—it's something you receive. Not because you've earned it, but because love gave it freely. Not because you're worthy, but because He makes you worthy. Not because you're perfect, but because His love is.

You can trust Him because He said, "I have come." Not "I'm considering it" or "I might, if you try hard enough." He came. He arrived. He delivered. The abundant life is already here, already paid for, already yours.

The enemy steals. Jesus gives. The enemy kills. Jesus resurrects. The enemy destroys. Jesus restores. Every single time.

Jesus, help me reject the lie that I should settle for less than Your abundant life. You didn't come to improve my old story—You came to give me Yours. Fill me with Your peace. Root me in Your love. Let me live from the fullness You've freely given. Amen.""",

"09-08": """Jesus didn't let the disciples destroy what they didn't understand. Fire from heaven felt like justice to them—until He turned and redirected their hearts toward something better. This is what love does: it interrupts before damage is done.

Your heart is precious to Him. So precious that He won't let it harden with bitterness or the kind of anger that destroys rather than heals. When He redirects you, it's not disappointment—it's love protecting who you really are.

The disciples wanted to be like Elijah. But Jesus was shaping them to be like Him—bringing life, not death; peace, not destruction. That's the identity He's cultivating in you too. Not someone who destroys what hurts them, but someone who transforms it with grace.

Every time the Spirit gently says "there's a better way," that's not condemnation—that's love preserving the beauty He planted in you. You were made for mercy, not judgment. Designed for healing, not retaliation. Called to peace, not war.

You can trust His redirection because He always guides you toward who you truly are. The correction that feels like a restraint is actually a reminder of your identity as His beloved child—someone who carries peace, not destruction.

The world will tell you to fight fire with fire. Jesus calls you to something higher—to be so secure in His love that you can respond with grace even when others don't deserve it. That's not weakness. That's the most powerful thing in the world.

Jesus, thank You for loving me enough to redirect my heart when it wanders toward hardness. Your correction is always love guiding me back to who I really am—Your beloved, called to peace, shaped by grace. Amen.""",

"10-08": """The Creator of every star, every mountain, every ocean chose to have nowhere to rest His head. This wasn't poverty—this was radical love. Jesus didn't become homeless because He had to. He became homeless because you needed Him to.

Every fox has a den. Every bird has a nest. But the Son of Man—the One who formed the earth and carved out every valley—walked this world without a permanent address. Why? Because your homelessness in sin mattered more to Him than His comfort in heaven.

This is the staggering truth: you are so valuable to Jesus that He gave up everything—even basic shelter—to reach you. You are not an afterthought or a charity case. You are worth the sacrifice of the Son of God becoming a wanderer.

When you feel displaced, when you feel like you don't quite belong anywhere, remember: Jesus knows exactly how that feels. He chose that feeling so you would never experience spiritual homelessness again. In Him, you have found your true home.

You can trust Him because He proved His love by emptying His hands. He who owned everything owned nothing—so you could inherit all things. That kind of love doesn't abandon. It doesn't retreat. It stays.

The peace you're searching for isn't found in perfect circumstances or comfortable surroundings. It's found in knowing that the One with no place to lay His head has prepared a place for you—here, now, in His love.

Jesus, You gave up the comforts of heaven to find me when I was lost. Now I am home in You. When this world feels unstable, remind me that my true address is Your love. You are my rest, my peace, my eternal home. Amen.""",

"11-08": """Jesus sees what others miss—a world full of people ready to receive radical love, with not enough people willing to share it. And His invitation to you isn't a burden. It's an identity: you get to be part of the greatest love story ever told.

You were once part of that harvest. You were the one who felt lost, disconnected, searching for something real. And Jesus loved you so much that He sent someone—a conversation, a moment of grace, His Spirit drawing you—to bring you home.

Now you get to be that for someone else. Not because you're perfect or have all the answers, but because you've been radically loved and that love is too good to keep to yourself. This is simply overflow, not performance.

This calling doesn't require you to become someone different. It requires you to be who you already are—someone whose life has been changed by Jesus. When His love flows through you, you're not working to earn anything. You're sharing what you've already received.

You can trust this purpose because He carries the weight of it. You're not responsible for the outcome—only for the overflow. Jesus does the heart-changing work. You just get to be the vessel through which His love reaches someone who desperately needs it.

Every person you encounter is precious to Him. Every act of kindness is a seed. You don't have to be eloquent or impressive—you just have to be willing to let love flow.

Jesus, thank You for sending someone to reach me. Now help me see the world through Your eyes—full of people ready to receive Your love. Use me as Your vessel, not because I'm qualified, but because Your love in me is too good not to share. Amen.""",

"12-08": """The disciples came back amazed—not because they were suddenly powerful, but because they discovered the power they already had in Jesus' name. They hadn't become different people. They had simply learned who they were.

You carry that same authority. Not because you've earned it or achieved a certain level of spiritual maturity, but because Jesus has given it to you as part of your identity in Him. The name that gave the disciples victory gives you victory over every lie, every fear, every attack on your peace.

Every force that tries to steal your identity, kill your joy, or destroy your peace has to bow to the name of Jesus you carry. Anxiety doesn't get the final word in your life—Jesus does. Depression can't own your future—Jesus already has. Fear can't dictate your decisions—you belong to the Prince of Peace.

This authority isn't about becoming someone you're not. It's about walking in who you already are. You are a beloved child of the Most High God, and that identity comes with kingdom authority. You don't have to beg for victory—you can declare it.

You can trust this because Jesus didn't give you authority and then leave you to figure it out. He gave you His name, His Spirit, and His backing. When you speak in His name, the full weight of heaven stands behind it.

You are not a victim of circumstances. You are a victor through Christ—and there's a massive difference between those two identities.

Jesus, thank You for giving me authority in Your name. Help me walk in the victory You've already won. I don't fight for triumph—I fight from triumph. Your love has made me more than a conqueror, and nothing can stand against what You've declared over my life. Amen.""",

"13-08": """Jesus redirected their celebration from what they could do to who they were. Not to diminish their joy—but to anchor their identity in something far more stable than spiritual performance: His permanent, unshakeable love.

Your name is written in heaven. Not penciled in tentatively, waiting to see if you'll mess up. Not written in disappearing ink, dependent on your spiritual track record. It's permanently inscribed by Love Himself, sealed by the blood of Jesus, guaranteed by the faithfulness of God.

This is where real peace comes from—not from how well you're doing spiritually, but from how completely you're loved. Your worth isn't determined by your victories over the enemy. It's established by your position as God's beloved child.

Before you think about what you need to do for God today, remember what He's already done for you. Before you worry about whether you're faithful enough or strong enough, remember: your name is written in heaven because He is faithful enough.

You can trust this because it doesn't depend on you. Your name isn't maintained by your performance—it's secured by His promise. You don't have to earn your way in. You don't have to keep yourself there. You are already loved, already chosen, already secure.

When condemnation whispers that you're not doing enough—your name is written in heaven. When shame says you've disqualified yourself—your name is written in love. When fear suggests God might change His mind—His love is unchanging, and so is your place in His heart.

Jesus, my greatest joy isn't what I can do for You—it's what You've already done for me. My name is in heaven, not because I earned it, but because You freely gave it. Let me live from that secure, settled identity today. Amen.""",

"14-08": """When the expert asked about eternal life, Jesus pointed him to love—not as a requirement to earn life, but as the very essence of what life is. This isn't about performance. It's about identity. You were created to love and be loved by Jesus.

The life He's describing isn't just something that happens after you die. It's the abundant, peaceful, purposeful life available right now—when you grasp how deeply you're loved and let that love flow through you to others.

You don't love God to get Him to love you back. You love Him because His love has awakened love in your heart. You don't love others to earn your way into heaven. You love others because heaven has already made its way into you through Jesus.

This is your true identity: someone so radically loved that love has become your natural response to life. When you love God with everything, you're not checking off a religious requirement—you're living from the fullness of knowing how completely He loves you.

You can trust this because love is not a law you have to obey in your own strength. It's a life He placed inside you. The Spirit of God, who is love, lives within you. Love isn't your assignment—it's your nature.

When you struggle to love difficult people, remember: you're not drawing from your own limited supply but from His unlimited reservoir. When you feel like you're not loving God enough, remember: He's celebrating His love for you, not measuring yours for Him.

Jesus, help me understand that love isn't what I do to earn life—love is the life You've given me. Fill me so full of Your peace that loving others becomes as natural as breathing. Root me in Your love and let it overflow. Amen.""",

"15-08": """The expert was looking for the boundaries of love—who counts and who doesn't. He wanted to make it manageable. But Jesus was about to show him that radical love doesn't have edges. It has overflow.

This question reveals the human heart's desire to contain love, to make it convenient. We want to love people who are easy to love, who look like us, who treat us well. But Jesus calls us to a love that mirrors His own—boundless, unconditional, without walls.

You are loved this way by Jesus. His love for you doesn't have limits based on your performance, your past, or your potential. He doesn't love you because you're loveable—He loves you because He is love. And this same radical love, He calls you to share.

Your neighbor isn't just the person next door. Your neighbor is the difficult coworker, the one who hurt you, the stranger who needs help. Anyone God places in your path who needs what you've already received.

You can trust His call because the love He asks you to give isn't something you have to manufacture. It flows from His supply, not yours. When you know how completely you're accepted by Jesus, you can offer that same acceptance to others.

The love Jesus calls you to isn't draining—it's life-giving. It doesn't come from your own limited reserves but from His unlimited presence in you. You love not because you have to, but because His love in you can't help but overflow.

Jesus, You loved me when I was unloveable, reached me when I was unreachable, and chose me when I was unchosen. Help me love with that same radical love—not from obligation, but from overflow. Show me who You want to love through me today. Amen.""",

"16-08": """Martha's heart was in the right place, but her focus was off. She was working to honor Jesus while missing the truth that He had already honored her with His presence. Her frantic service was trying to earn what she already had.

This is the beautiful tension: you don't serve Jesus to gain His love. You serve because you already have it. Your identity isn't found in how much you do for God but in how much He's already done for you. Martha was distracted by preparations. Mary was absorbed in His presence.

Jesus didn't come to Martha's house to be impressed by her hospitality. He came because He loved being with her. He doesn't need your performance to validate your worth—your worth was already established in His radical love, before you did a single thing.

When you're running around trying to prove your devotion, Jesus is saying what He said to Martha: "You are worried and upset about many things, but only one thing is needed." That one thing isn't more service. It's deeper intimacy with the One who calls you beloved.

You can trust His invitation because He says it with love, not criticism. He's not disappointed in Martha—He's protecting her from missing the moment. He protects you the same way, calling you back to the place where peace actually lives.

Your identity isn't "servant trying to earn love." It's "beloved child who serves from love." There's a world of difference between those two people—and Jesus wants you to be the second one.

Jesus, help me remember that You don't love me because of what I do—You love me because of who I am to You. Teach me to sit at Your feet first, to receive before I give. My identity is found in being Your beloved, not in being busy for You. Amen.""",

"17-08": """While the world obsesses over fading strength and aging bodies, Jesus is doing something magnificent inside you that no mirror can reflect. Every day that your outward self grows older, your inward self—the real you—is being renewed by His radical love.

This isn't positive thinking. It's a supernatural transformation happening in the deepest part of your identity. While culture says your value decreases with time, Jesus says the opposite: your true self is increasing daily as you become more like Him.

Every challenge that feels like it's wearing you down is building you up spiritually. Every difficulty that seems to break you is making you stronger in the places that matter most. The real you—the part that bears God's image, the part that will live forever—is getting more whole every day.

Your identity isn't tied to energy levels, appearance, or physical capability. You are a spiritual being having a human experience. And while your body shows the effects of time, your soul shows the effects of grace. Grace wins.

You can trust this because Paul wasn't writing theory—he was writing from prison, in chains, while physically suffering. He had proof. The renewal is real. The inward life is stronger than what any outward circumstance can touch.

When you see the evidence of time in the mirror, remember: that's not the real you. The real you is being made glorious by the One who loves you radically. Your outer shell is temporary. Your inner reality is eternal.

Jesus, while the world sees aging, You see renewal. Help me remember that my true identity is found in the part of me You are making new every day. I am not my body—I am Your beloved, being transformed into Your likeness. Amen.""",

"18-08": """Gideon was hiding. Afraid, beaten down, convinced he was the least of the least. But when God looked at him, He didn't see a coward—He saw a mighty warrior. This was God declaring Gideon's true identity, the one that fear and failure had buried.

You might be hiding too. In insecurity, in disappointment, in the belief that you're just not enough. But when Jesus looks at you, He doesn't see your current struggles—He sees who you really are. He sees past your fears to your faith, past your weakness to your calling.

God's love doesn't define you by where you are. It defines you by who you are. Gideon thought he was defined by his circumstances—powerless, afraid, overlooked. But God defined him by his destiny. Mighty warrior. Deliverer. Overcomer.

This is the radical love that changes everything: Jesus doesn't love you for who you might become if you get your act together. He loves you for who you already are in His eyes. Your identity is found in His permanent perspective, not your temporary performance.

You can trust this because God didn't lie to Gideon—He delivered on every word. The man who hid in a winepress led an army and changed a nation. What God declares over you, He also delivers.

Stop hiding in insecurity. The One who sees you has called you mighty—and He's never been wrong about anyone He's called.

Lord, help me see myself through Your eyes. When I feel weak and hidden, remind me that You see me as mighty and chosen. My identity isn't determined by my circumstances—it's determined by Your radical love. I am who You say I am. Amen.""",

"19-08": """Before Gideon could step into his calling, he had to deal with the false gods in his own backyard. This wasn't about earning God's love—it was about removing everything that competed with it. God's love for Gideon was so fierce, He wouldn't share his heart with counterfeits.

He's asking you the same question: what has taken His place? Not in condemnation—in love. Because He knows that whatever sits on the throne of your heart shapes everything else. And He knows no counterfeit can deliver what you really need.

Your altars might not be wooden idols, but they can be just as destructive. The approval of others. Financial security. A relationship that's become everything. Success, comfort, self-image. Whatever you turn to first when you're afraid—that's your altar.

Jesus doesn't demand first place because He's controlling. He wants first place because He's the only one who can actually deliver what all those other things promise—peace, security, identity, love. Everything else is a pale imitation.

You can trust His priority because He is not asking you to give up something good. He's asking you to make room for something infinitely better. When Jesus is first, everything else finds its proper place. Your peace becomes unshakeable. Your vision clears.

Tearing down false altars isn't a loss—it's liberation. You were made to worship, and you'll never be at rest until you're worshiping the right One.

Jesus, show me what has quietly taken Your place in my heart. I don't want counterfeits when I can have the real King. Help me tear down whatever competes with Your love and build my life entirely on You. You alone deserve first place. Amen.""",

"20-08": """God told Gideon he had too many soldiers. From a human perspective, this made no sense—they needed every advantage. But God had a different plan: He wanted the victory to be unmistakably His, not a product of human strategy.

This is the radical love of Jesus at work—He would rather you experience His supernatural power than trust in your own limited ability. He doesn't reduce your resources to hurt you. He removes them so you can discover what only He can do.

Maybe you feel like your army has been reduced. The people you counted on walked away. The resources dried up. The abilities you relied on aren't enough anymore. This isn't abandonment—this is love positioning you for a miracle.

Your identity isn't found in your human capacity to figure things out or muster enough support. It's found in being loved by the God who can do infinitely more than you could ever ask or imagine. He specializes in impossible odds.

You can trust Him because Gideon's 300 proved it. They didn't win because they were mighty warriors. They won because they served a mighty God. The same God. The same power. The same love for you.

Stop trying to build a bigger army. Start trusting a bigger God. His love for you is so fierce that He'll reduce your human options to increase your supernatural possibilities.

Lord, when I feel like I don't have enough resources or strength, remind me that I have You. My victory doesn't come from what I can gather but from what You provide. Help me trust Your power over my own ability—You are always enough. Amen.""",

"21-08": """David had to remind his own soul to remember God's benefits. Even the man after God's own heart had to fight forgetfulness. This isn't a character flaw—it's human nature. And remembering how God has loved you isn't nostalgia. It's survival.

Every benefit you've received is evidence of His radical love. Every time He healed you, provided for you, protected you, guided you—that wasn't luck. That was love in action. When you remember these moments, you're not just being grateful. You're reminding yourself who you are to Him.

Forgetfulness is one of the enemy's favorite weapons. When you forget how God has loved you in the past, you start doubting His love in the present. When you forget His faithfulness yesterday, you start fearing tomorrow. Remembrance is your defense.

Your identity is built on the foundation of His benefits—forgiveness, healing, redemption, love, mercy. These aren't just nice things that happened to you. They're declarations of your worth in His eyes. Every blessing is Him saying, "You matter to Me."

You can trust Him because His track record is written into your own story. He has shown up before. He is the same God today. His character hasn't shifted. His love hasn't cooled. His faithfulness hasn't expired.

When anxiety tries to convince you that God has forgotten you, remember His benefits. When fear suggests He won't come through this time, remember all the times He already has. The evidence is all around you.

Jesus, help me remember all the ways You've loved me. When I'm tempted to forget Your goodness, remind me of Your benefits. My peace comes from remembering how consistently You have shown up for me—and You are not finished yet. Amen.""",

"22-08": """The disciples watched Jesus pray, and something about it was different. It wasn't the length or the eloquence—it was the intimacy. They could see He wasn't talking to the sky. He was having a conversation with Someone who deeply loved Him. And they wanted that too.

That's what they asked for. Not religious technique or impressive vocabulary. Connection. They saw Jesus talk to His Father like a beloved child talks to a perfect parent, and they realized—this could be theirs.

This is the radical love of Jesus: He didn't keep that intimacy for Himself. He invites you into the same relationship with the Father that He has. Prayer isn't about getting God's attention—you already have it. It's about living from that reality.

Your identity as His child means you have unlimited access to the Father's heart. You don't have to pray perfectly or for a certain amount of time. You just have to come. Like a child running to a loving parent, you can approach God with total confidence, knowing you're always welcome.

You can trust this because Jesus Himself taught it. The One who knows the Father most intimately said, "When you pray, say Father." That word alone changes everything. You're not petitioning a distant judge—you're talking to a Father who delights in you.

Stop trying to impress God with your prayers. Start enjoying Him in your prayers. He's not waiting for you to get it right—He's waiting for you to get real.

Father, thank You for inviting me into conversation with You. Help me remember that prayer isn't performance—it's relationship. I don't have to earn Your attention. I already have it, because I am Your beloved child. Teach me to pray from that place. Amen.""",

"23-08": """Jesus used the most relatable comparison possible: parental love. Even imperfect parents want to give good gifts to their children. But God's love makes human parental love look small—because the gift He wants to give you isn't just good. It's Himself.

The Holy Spirit isn't a spiritual bonus feature. He is the presence of God living inside you. This is radical love taken to its ultimate expression: God doesn't love you from a distance. He comes to live within you. You carry the presence of the One who spoke the universe into existence.

This transforms your identity completely. You're not just someone God loves—you're someone God inhabits. Not just forgiven, but filled. Not just accepted, but indwelt by the very presence of Love Himself. That changes everything about who you are.

When you ask for the Holy Spirit, you're not asking for an experience or a feeling. You're asking for God to make His home in you. And He is more willing to give you Himself than you are to receive Him. His love is that radical, that generous, that complete.

You can trust this because Jesus promised it without conditions. Not "I'll give the Holy Spirit to the spiritually qualified." He said, "How much more will your Father in heaven give the Holy Spirit to those who ask." You ask. He gives. That's the deal.

When you feel alone, remember: the Holy Spirit is with you. When you feel weak, His strength is in you. When you feel unloved, Love Himself has made His home in your heart.

Father, thank You for not just loving me from heaven but for living within me through Your Holy Spirit. I'm not just loved by You—I'm filled with You. Let me live from that reality today. Amen.""",

"24-08": """The Pharisee was shocked that Jesus skipped the ritual washing before eating. In his world, spiritual cleanness was measured by external actions. Jesus was about to shatter that entire framework—because God's love isn't drawn to religious performance. It's drawn to authentic hearts.

This is liberating news for your identity: you don't have to perform perfectly to be loved perfectly. Jesus isn't keeping score of your spiritual rituals, tracking your Bible reading streaks, or measuring your prayer time. He's looking at your heart.

The Pharisee's shock reveals how easily we replace real relationship with religious routine. You can do all the "right" Christian things and feel distant from God if your heart isn't genuinely engaged. But you can mess up externally and still be deeply connected to Him if your heart is honestly seeking Him.

Jesus loves you for who you are, not for what you do. Your identity isn't found in how well you perform religious duties—it's found in how completely you're loved by the One who sees past every external layer to the real you.

You can trust this because He came for the tax collectors and sinners before He came for the Pharisees. The ones who knew they didn't have it together received Him most easily. Religious performance was never the point—relationship always was.

Stop trying to impress God with external religion. Bring Him your actual heart. He already knows what's in it—and He loves you anyway.

Jesus, thank You for loving my heart more than my performance. Help me stop trying to earn Your love through religious activity and start receiving it through authentic relationship. You see past every external layer, and what You see, You love. Amen.""",

"25-08": """The Pharisees were precise about tithing garden herbs and completely missing the point of their entire faith. They measured everything small and neglected everything large. Jesus wasn't impressed—and this is His gentle warning to you too.

It's possible to focus so hard on religious activities that you miss what God actually wants—connection with Him and love toward others. Your identity isn't found in how perfectly you follow spiritual rules. It's found in how deeply you receive and pour out His radical love.

God isn't impressed by perfectionism disconnected from justice and mercy. He'd rather you show up imperfectly with a heart full of love than perform perfectly with a heart that's grown cold. The heart is always the point.

This brings real freedom: you don't have to major in the minors. You don't have to stress about getting every spiritual discipline perfect while neglecting the command to simply love God and love others. Love is the main thing. Everything else flows from there.

You can trust His priority because it's where He Himself lives. God is love. When you pursue love above performance, you're not abandoning spiritual discipline—you're discovering what spiritual discipline was always supposed to produce.

When you find yourself getting tangled in religious performance, let this verse pull you back to center. Love. Justice. Mercy. The big things are the main things.

Jesus, help me keep the main thing the main thing. When I get caught up in spiritual performance, bring me back to what matters most—loving You and loving others. My identity is found in Your love, not in perfecting my religious routine. Amen.""",

"26-08": """Jesus isn't trying to frighten you into obedience—He's trying to free you from the wrong kind of fear. The world offers countless things to be afraid of: rejection, failure, loss. But when you understand who holds ultimate authority, every lesser fear loses its power.

This isn't about being terrified of God. It's about being so secure in His radical love that earthly fears lose their grip on you. When the One with ultimate power also loves you unconditionally, what exactly can this world do to you?

Your identity isn't found in avoiding every threat—it's found in trusting the One who has authority over every outcome. The "fear" of God isn't terror. It's awe-filled reverence for the Being who chose to love you before you even existed, and whose love will outlast every circumstance you'll ever face.

Think about it: if the God who holds authority over life and death calls you His beloved child, what can temporary circumstances actually touch? If the One who controls eternity has written your name in heaven, why would you fear those who can only affect the temporary?

You can trust this because perfect love casts out fear—and His love for you is perfect. Not your love for Him, but His love for you. That love is complete, unchanging, and more powerful than every fear trying to hold you down.

The right kind of awe—reverencing the God who loves you most—dissolves every wrong kind of fear. You are held by the One who has the final say over everything.

Father, help me remember that the same hands holding ultimate authority are the hands holding me in love. When earthly fears try to overwhelm me, remind me that You are bigger than all of them. My security is found in trusting You. Amen.""",

"27-08": """Jesus is looking for faithful managers—and He's looking at you. This isn't about earning His trust. This is about recognizing that He's already given it. The fact that He's entrusted you with anything at all is proof of how deeply He believes in you.

Your identity as His beloved child means you're also His trusted steward. He doesn't give you responsibilities to overwhelm you—He gives them to show you how much He believes in you. Every role you have, every gift you've been given—that's Jesus saying, "I trust you with this."

This changes everything about how you approach your day. You're not just trying to get through another week. You're stewarding what the King of the universe has placed in your hands. You're not managing tasks; you're caring for what belongs to the One who loves you most.

The peace in this calling comes from knowing your worth isn't determined by how perfectly you manage everything. It's determined by the fact that He chose to trust you in the first place. You don't have to be perfect to be faithful. You just have to be willing.

You can trust this responsibility because He equips what He entrusts. He doesn't hand you a calling and disappear. He remains with you, guiding you, strengthening you, faithful to complete in you what He started.

You are not just a servant trying to earn approval. You are a beloved child trusted with the family business. That's your identity—loved and trusted by the One who owns everything.

Lord, thank You for trusting me with pieces of Your heart and Your kingdom. Help me be faithful—not to earn Your love, but because I'm secure in it. Show me how to steward what You've given me with the same love You've shown me. Amen.""",

"28-08": """Every blessing in your life is Jesus saying, "I believe in you." Your gifts, your opportunities, your relationships—these aren't random luck or coincidence. They're expressions of His radical love and His confidence in exactly who you are.

This isn't about pressure. It's about purpose. God doesn't bless you to burden you—He blesses you so His love can flow through you to others. Your identity as His beloved child means you're also His chosen vessel to carry His heart into the world.

The "much" that's been given to you isn't only the obvious things. It's the grace you've experienced, the hope you've been given, the peace you've found in Him. And because you've been so radically loved, much will be asked—not to earn more love, but to share the love you've already received.

This brings freedom: you're not hoarding blessings to secure yourself. You're stewarding them to help others find security in God's love. You're not protecting what's yours. You're sharing what's His through you. There's a massive difference.

You can trust this purpose because He never assigns responsibility without grace to carry it. When you feel overwhelmed by what's expected, remember: He doesn't give you more than He'll also give you grace to steward well.

You are blessed to be a blessing. That's not pressure—that's your purpose. And living from that purpose is where real joy is found.

Jesus, thank You for blessing me not just for my own benefit, but so I can bless others. Help me see every gift as an opportunity to share Your love. I don't want to hoard what You've given me—I want to steward it generously, for Your glory and others' good. Amen.""",

"29-08": """Jesus isn't just giving advice on conflict resolution—He's revealing your identity as a peacemaker. You're not called to win arguments. You're called to win hearts. This is who you are as someone radically loved by the Prince of Peace.

Reconciliation isn't about admitting you're wrong when you're right. It's about valuing relationship more than being right. And your identity as His beloved child means you can afford to make the first move—because your worth isn't determined by who apologizes first.

When Jesus pursued you while you were still His enemy, He showed you what love looks like in action. He didn't wait for you to get your life together. He made the first move. Now He's calling you to do the same with others.

This is the peace you carry—not the absence of conflict, but the presence of Someone who can heal any relationship. You don't reconcile because you have to. You reconcile because you get to. You've been so radically loved that you can love others from overflow.

You can trust His call because He leads from example. He crossed every gap, climbed every wall, and broke every barrier to reach you. The One asking you to reconcile is the same One who reconciled you to Himself at the highest possible cost.

You are not a victim of broken relationships. You are an agent of reconciliation—and that is one of the most powerful identities in the universe.

Jesus, You reconciled me to Yourself while I was still Your enemy. Help me be a reconciler too. Give me courage to move first, humility to release the need to be right, and love that values people over positions. Let Your peace flow through me to heal broken places. Amen.""",

"30-08": """For eighteen years, this woman was bent over, bound, and forgotten by most. But when Jesus saw her, He didn't see a problem—He saw His daughter. "Daughter of Abraham," He called her, declaring that her identity was never found in her condition but in her belonging.

That's your identity too. Not defined by what's broken in your life, but by whose you are. You are not your struggle, your pain, or your limitation. You are His daughter, His son, His beloved child—and that identity is stronger than any chain that tries to hold you.

Jesus didn't wait for the right moment according to religious rules. His love doesn't follow human schedules or cultural expectations. When He sees His children in bondage, love compels Him to act—even on the Sabbath, even when others object, even when it's inconvenient for the crowd.

Maybe you've been bent over by shame or bound by fear. Eighteen years might feel like forever, but it's nothing to the One who holds eternity. Your breakthrough isn't late—it was always meant for this moment.

You can trust Him because He sees your identity before He addresses your condition. He called her "daughter" before He touched her. He calls you beloved before He changes your circumstances. The love comes first. Always.

When religion says wait, love says now. When others see your condition, Jesus sees your name. You belong to Him—and that belonging is already your freedom.

Jesus, thank You for seeing past my condition to my identity. I am not defined by what binds me—I am defined by who I belong to. Set me free from anything keeping me bent over. I am Your beloved child, and in that truth, I find my freedom. Amen.""",

"31-08": """You woke up this morning searching for something. That ache—the restless feeling that whispers you're not enough, that you need to achieve more, become more—is the loudest lie the enemy tells. And today, the Word of God cuts through it completely.

In Christ, you have already been brought to fullness. Not will be. Not might be if you try harder. You have been. Present reality. Finished fact. Settled forever.

This isn't spiritual wishful thinking—it's your actual position before God. The moment you said yes to Jesus, heaven's verdict was pronounced: Complete. Whole. Lacking nothing. Not because of what you've done, but because of where you stand—in Him.

Think about what this means. The One who holds supreme authority over every power and principality, every force in the universe—He lives in you. His completeness becomes your completeness. His victory, your victory. His identity, your identity.

You can trust this because Paul wrote it to ordinary people living ordinary lives. Not to spiritual giants, but to believers just like you. The completeness he describes is the birthright of everyone who belongs to Jesus. Including you. Especially you.

You don't need to chase wholeness through achievements or relationships. The search is over. The verdict is in. You are complete in Christ—and that changes everything about how you face today.

Jesus, I receive this truth into the depths of my being. I am complete in You—not partially, not eventually, but fully and finally. Let this reality reshape how I see myself and how I move through this world today. I lack nothing because I have everything in You. Amen."""

}

# Load the full JSON
with open('SpeakLife/Preview Content/AffirmationData/devotionals.json', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    data = json.load(f)

devs = data['devotionals']

updated = 0
for d in devs:
    date = d.get('date', '')
    if date in REWRITES:
        d['devotionalText'] = REWRITES[date]
        updated += 1

data['devotionals'] = devs

with open('SpeakLife/Preview Content/AffirmationData/devotionals.json', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    json.dump(data, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)

print(f"Updated {updated} entries.")
